### What is the Longest Common Subsequence?

A subsequence is a sequence of characters taken from a string in their original order, but not necessarily contiguously. The Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) of two strings is the longest such sequence that appears in both. For example, for strings `ABCDE` and `ACE`, the LCS is `ACE` with length 3.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// String A: "TRANSFER"
// String B: "TRANSFORM"
// LCS: "TRANSF" with some variations
// Characters appear in both in the same order, not necessarily adjacent
```

### Why is LCS important?

LCS is the foundation for many string comparison problems. Unix `diff` tools use LCS to find what is common between two file versions. Edit distance (Levenshtein distance) is closely related: the minimum edits equal `m + n - 2 * LCS`. The shortest common supersequence length equals `m + n - LCS`. Understanding LCS unlocks this entire family of two-string dynamic programs.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Relationships:
// Edit distance (insertions + deletions only) = m + n - 2 * LCS
// Shortest common supersequence length = m + n - LCS
// Diff tool output = characters NOT in the LCS
```

### How does the 2D DP table work?

You build a table of size `(m+1) x (n+1)` where `m` and `n` are the lengths of the two strings. The zero-th row and zero-th column are all zeros, representing the LCS of any prefix with an empty string. You fill the table row by row. When the characters at position `i` in A and position `j` in B match, both characters extend the subsequence, so you take the diagonal value plus one. When they differ, you cannot extend, so you take the better of dropping the last character of A or B.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LCSCalculator {

    public int longestCommonSubsequence(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LCSCalculator calc = new LCSCalculator();
        System.out.println(calc.longestCommonSubsequence("ABCDE", "ACE"));
        System.out.println(calc.longestCommonSubsequence("TRANSFER", "TRANSFORM"));
    }
}
```

```text
3
7
```

### How do you reconstruct the actual subsequence?

To find the actual LCS string, not just the length, you backtrack from `dp[m][n]`. When the characters at position `i` and `j` match, include that character and move diagonally to `dp[i-1][j-1]`. When they differ, move toward the larger of `dp[i-1][j]` or `dp[i][j-1]`. Collect characters in reverse and then reverse the result.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LCSReconstructor {

    public String findLCS(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }

        // Backtrack to find the actual subsequence
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        int i = m, j = n;
        while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
            if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                sb.append(a.charAt(i - 1));
                i--;
                j--;
            } else if (dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]) {
                i--;
            } else {
                j--;
            }
        }
        return sb.reverse().toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LCSReconstructor recon = new LCSReconstructor();
        System.out.println(recon.findLCS("ABCDE", "ACE"));
    }
}
```

```text
ACE
```

### How do you optimize space with a rolling row?

Because each row of the DP table only depends on the previous row, you can use two 1D arrays instead of a full 2D table. This reduces space from O(m * n) to O(min(m, n)) by making the shorter string the column dimension.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LCSSpaceOptimized {

    public int longestCommonSubsequence(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() < b.length()) {
            String temp = a; a = b; b = temp; // ensure b is shorter
        }
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[] prev = new int[n + 1];
        int[] curr = new int[n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    curr[j] = prev[j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    curr[j] = Math.max(prev[j], curr[j - 1]);
                }
            }
            int[] temp = prev;
            prev = curr;
            curr = temp;
            java.util.Arrays.fill(curr, 0);
        }
        return prev[n];
    }
}
```

### How is LCS different from Longest Common Substring?

LCS allows gaps between matched characters (non-contiguous), while Longest Common Substring requires the match to be contiguous. The table structure looks similar but the mismatch rule differs: LCS inherits from neighbors, while Longest Common Substring resets to zero.

| Aspect | Longest Common Subsequence | Longest Common Substring |
|---|---|---|
| Contiguity | Characters need not be adjacent | Characters must be adjacent |
| Mismatch rule | max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]) | 0 (reset) |
| Answer location | Bottom-right corner dp[m][n] | Global maximum over all cells |
| Example: ABCDE, ABFCE | LCS = ABCE (length 4) | Substring = AB (length 2) |

